  "read": {
    "headline": "One trade is setting the price of everything else",
    "standfirst": "Energy is up sixty-nine percent on the year while the index is up twelve, the belly of the curve is repricing for rate rises, and long-duration equity is paying for both. Almost every other spread on this page is downstream of that.",
    "points": [
      {
        "title": "Equities are not selling off. Long-duration equities are.",
        "text": "Energy is the best sector on every window past three months and the only one up on the day. Consumer Discretionary and Communication Services are negative year to date while the index is up double digits. That is not a growth scare — a growth scare takes energy down with it. It is a rate shock working through the discount rate, and it hits the assets whose value sits furthest in the future."
      },
      {
        "title": "The curve says hikes, not term premium.",
        "text": "Over the past month the two- to five-year part of the curve rose about five percent while the twenty- and thirty-year moved less than one. When the front and belly lead, the market is repricing the policy path. When the long end leads, it is repricing risk. This is the first, and it is why the twenty-year Treasury ETF is down over six percent on the year while the one-to-three-year is down under two."
      },
      {
        "title": "Value over growth is a seventeen-point spread, and it is a rates trade.",
        "text": "Value is nine percent ahead of the S&P year to date; growth is eight percent behind. Trading these as separate ideas misses that they are one position expressed twice. If you are long value and short growth you are short duration in equity form, and you will be right and wrong on the same days the bond market is."
      },
      {
        "title": "“Emerging markets” has stopped being one thing.",
        "text": "Korea is up ninety-six percent year to date and Taiwan seventy-six, while China is down eleven, India down ten and Indonesia down thirty. The EM index is up twenty-five, which describes none of them. A basket whose components disagree this violently is a basket you should not be trading as a basket."
      },
      {
        "title": "The dollar is not confirming the rate story.",
        "text": "Yields at three-year highs would normally pull the dollar up with them. Instead the Australian dollar is up eight percent against it this year and the Korean won seven. Gold is roughly flat on the year despite the geopolitics. When rates and the dollar disagree, one of them is early — and that disagreement is the cleanest thing on this page to have an opinion about."
      },
      {
        "title": "Crypto has decoupled on the year and recoupled on the month.",
        "text": "Bitcoin is down twelve percent year to date against an S&P up twelve, but up twenty-three over the past month. If you carry crypto exposure alongside index positions and size it as a diversifier, check the correlation you are actually running rather than the one you assumed."
      }
    ],
    "howToUse": "Do not trade the cells. Trade the disagreements between them. A page like this earns its keep on the days two rows that normally move together stop moving together — that is where a thesis lives. Everything here is a snapshot of a close, not a live quote."
  },
